var a=[{title:`The adaptation cycle`,text:`A workout is a stimulus. Your muscles produce force, your heart and blood vessels deliver more oxygen, and you practice movement skills. Right afterward, fatigue can lower your performance for a while. During the recovery that follows, your body repairs, refills its stores and adapts. You notice the result over weeks and months of repeated cycles.`},{title:`Specific adaptations`,text:`The SAID principle (Specific Adaptation to Imposed Demands) means your body adapts mainly to what you practice. Heavy squats improve force production in squat-like patterns, and brisk walking mainly trains aerobic capacity. General fitness combines several kinds of challenge. Early strength gains often come from better coordination and nervous-system recruitment, while noticeable muscle growth usually takes longer.`},{title:`Supercompensation`,text:`A popular diagram shows fitness dropping after training, recovering, then briefly rising above where it started. It is a useful teaching picture, but it is not a precise timetable or a universal biological law. Different systems recover at different rates, and you don’t need to time each session to a mythical peak.`},{title:`Example`,text:`A beginner does two moderate full-body workouts a week. After several sessions, the same exercises feel easier. They add one or two reps, a little weight or another carefully chosen challenge while keeping their technique. If they keep stacking hard sessions without recovery, their progress may stall.`},{title:`Using TrainUp`,text:`Pick a primary goal in Profile, check off your sets during each workout and rate your effort afterward. History shows a 4-week chart, so look at trends across several weeks. Before you make training harder, think about your sleep, soreness and how consistent you have been.`}];var r=[{label:`2026 ACSM position stand`,url:`https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41843416/`},{label:`WHO physical activity guidelines`,url:`https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9219310/`}];var o={sections:a,sources:r};export{o as default,a as sections,r as sources};